{"tool_name":"sweetpea","contents":["oeb","bioschemas","biotools"],"fetched_metadata":{"biotools":{"id":"sweetpea","home":"https://sweetpea-org.github.io/","license":"MIT","summary":"SweetPea is a domain-specific programming language built for the declarative specification of randomized experimental designs and the synthesis of trial sequences generated from those design specifications.","addition_date":"2021-12-13T17:52:56.106786Z","last_update_date":"2021-12-13T17:52:56.109221Z","tool_type":["Command-line tool"],"accessibility":"Open access","biotools_curie":"biotools:sweetpea","cost":"Free of charge","credit":[{"email":"musslick@princeton.edu","name":"Sebastian Musslick","orcidid":"https://orcid.org/0000-0002-8896-639X"},{"name":"Anastasia Cherkaev"},{"name":"Ben Draut"},{"name":"Jonathan D. Cohen"}],"documentation":[{"type":["API documentation"],"url":"https://sweetpea-org.github.io/api.html"},{"type":["User manual"],"url":"https://sweetpea-org.github.io/guide.html"}],"edit_permission":{"type":"private"},"function":[{"operation":[{"term":"Standardisation and normalisation","uri":"http://edamontology.org/operation_3435"}]}],"name":"SweetPea","operating_system":["Linux","Mac","Windows"],"owner":"Jennifer","publication":[{"doi":"10.3758/S13428-021-01598-2","metadata":{"abstract":"\u00a9 2021, The Psychonomic Society, Inc.Experimental design is a key ingredient of reproducible empirical research. Yet, given the increasing complexity of experimental designs, researchers often struggle to implement ones that allow them to measure their variables of interest without confounds. SweetPea (https://sweetpea-org.github.io/) is an open-source declarative language in Python, in which researchers can describe their desired experiment as a set of factors and constraints. The language leverages advances in areas of computer science to sample experiment sequences in an unbiased way. In this article, we provide an overview of SweetPea\u2019s capabilities, and demonstrate its application to the design of psychological experiments. Finally, we discuss current limitations of SweetPea, as well as potential applications to other domains of empirical research, such as neuroscience and machine learning.","authors":[{"name":"Butt A.S."},{"name":"Cherkaev A."},{"name":"Cohen J.D."},{"name":"Darragh P."},{"name":"Draut B."},{"name":"Flatt M."},{"name":"Musslick S."},{"name":"Srikumar V."}],"date":"2021-01-01T00:00:00Z","journal":"Behavior Research Methods","title":"SweetPea: A standard language for factorial experimental design"},"pmid":"34357537"}],"topic":[{"term":"Experimental design and studies","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3678"},{"term":"Machine learning","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3474"},{"term":"Neurobiology","uri":"http://edamontology.org/topic_3304"}],"validated":1},"bioschemas":{"name":"SweetPea","home":"https://bio.tools/sweetpea","license":"MIT","summary":"SweetPea is a domain-specific programming language built for the declarative specification of randomized experimental designs and the synthesis of trial sequences generated from those design specifications.","tool_type":"sc:SoftwareApplication"},"oeb":{"metrics":[{"@id":"https://openebench.bsc.es/monitor/metrics/biotools:sweetpea/cmd/sweetpea-org.github.io","@license":"https://creativecommons.org/licenses/by/4.0/","@nmsp":"biotools","@type":"metrics","deprecated":true,"distribution":{"binaries":{"freeness":null,"registration":null},"packages":null,"source_packages":false,"sourcecode":{"free":null,"interpreted":true,"license":null,"public":true,"readme":false,"repository":{"anonymous":null,"online":null}}},"project":{"build":{},"deployment":{},"documentation":{"api":true,"manual":true},"license":{"open_source":true,"osi":true},"publications":[{"entries":[{"cit_count":2,"citations":[{"count":1,"year":"2025"},{"count":1,"year":"2026"}],"doi":"10.3758/s13428-021-01598-2","pmid":"34357537","ref_count":25,"refs":[{"count":1,"year":"1987"},{"count":1,"year":"1990"},{"count":1